# What Prettier is not allowed to touch, and why.
#
# Formatting admits no legitimate exception, so this file is short on purpose and
# every entry names a reason. An ignore with no reason is a defect deferred.

# Not ours to format. npm owns the lockfile's shape and rewrites it on every
# install; a formatter fighting it would churn on every dependency change.
package-lock.json
node_modules/
**/node_modules/
.gomodcache/
apps/server_core/.gomodcache/
dist/
build/
.next/
coverage/
.gate/

# Generated. The authority is the generator, not this file, and reformatting the
# output makes the regen-diff check red for a reason unrelated to the schema.
apps/web/src/api/generated/
packages/sdk-runtime/src/generated/

# Captured output, in JSON rather than markdown. The rule is the same one the
# vendored-tree block below states: what a tool or a provider actually returned
# is evidence, and evidence that has been reflowed is no longer verbatim.
#
#   - testdata/ under the provider adapters holds recorded Mercado Livre response
#     bodies -- item multiget, order, shipment. Their value is that they are what
#     the API sent.
#   - docs/research/evidence/ and docs/marketplaces/ are captured crawler and API
#     output; several are still wrapped in the caller's `{"success":true,"data":…}`
#     envelope, which is the giveaway that nobody authored them by hand.
#
# Governance JSON under contracts/governance/ is deliberately NOT here: those are
# declarations this repository authors, and a formatter is welcome to them.
**/testdata/
docs/research/evidence/
docs/marketplaces/

# The published API contract, and this one was found the hard way.
#
# Running Prettier over it rewrote 839 lines: every `$ref: '#/...'` became
# `$ref: "#/..."`, plus reflow. Two Go contract-parity tests then went red --
# TestRefreshListingsOpenAPIContractParity and
# TestMarketCollectVerdictOpenAPIContractParity, both in `root_test.go`, which
# assert the literal text of this file. The formatter and the parity tests are
# two instruments over one document, and that is how they come to disagree.
#
# The spec is also the authority for the SDK and for the gate's route/spec/SDK
# equality checks. A cosmetic rewrite of an authority is not
# cosmetic. Its shape is owned by whoever edits the contract, not by this
# formatter.
contracts/api/*.yaml

# Markdown, all of it. This started as an exclusion of one evidence tree, and the
# measurement widened it.
#
# Prettier's markdown printer cannot re-indent a line that sits inside an inline
# code span spanning a line break, so it strips the indentation instead. In a
# nested list that moves the continuation line to column 0 and lifts it out of
# the list -- a change to document structure, not to formatting. Found first in a
# nested list of that kind, then measured across the whole scope on 2026-08-08:
# 77 of the 236 markdown files Prettier wanted to rewrite show the pattern.
#
# A formatter that damages one document in three cannot run unattended over a
# ratified profile, an ADR, or an audit record. Markdown therefore has no
# formatting gate here; that is a decision with a number behind it, not an
# oversight. Revisit if Prettier's markdown printer learns to preserve the
# indentation of wrapped code spans.
*.md

# Trees a formatter must not write to.
#
# `.mnfs/` was 1000 markdown files of mission artifacts, dispatch ledgers and
# measured output pasted verbatim from tools. The tree is retired and no longer
# tracked; the entry stays because a checkout that predates the removal still
# carries its gitignored run logs, and a formatter with write access to an audit
# trail is a worse problem than an unaligned table.
#
# The same reasoning, at lower stakes, covers the vendored agent/tooling trees:
# they are copies of upstream, and diffing them against upstream is easier when
# they have not been through our formatter.
.mnfs/
.claude/
.superpowers/
.agents/
